GOAL FRAME — Prove one bounded cross-functional workflow before adding provider-driven automation

1. OUTCOME (the one thing this run must prove)
One request enters the company, crosses at least two departments, and comes out the other side as a verified, evidence-backed deliverable — with every handoff, approval gate, and stop recorded by the runtime. If that holds once, end to end, the workflow is real. If it does not, no amount of provider-driven automation will make it real; automation would only multiply an unproven loop.

2. WHY THIS GOAL, NOW
The company already has the parts: 17 department agents, a runtime with revision/retry/maker-checker/evidence stops, an approval model, and a memory layer. What it does not yet have is a single recorded instance of those parts working together on one request from frame to verified close. The executor (AGENT-05) proved an agent can run and produce role-appropriate work. That is one leg, not one workflow. This run closes the gap between "an agent ran" and "the company delivered."

3. SCOPE — WHAT "BOUNDED" MEANS HERE
In scope:
- Exactly one request, chosen so it genuinely spans two or more departments.
- Sequential legs with a defined "done" per leg, written before dispatch.
- Runtime-recorded evidence at each step: input, owner, output, verification.
- At least one governance stop actually exercised (a 🟡 gate reached, surfaced, and held for human yes — not simulated away).
- One synthesized answer at the end, traceable back to this goal.

Out of scope (explicitly deferred, not forgotten):
- Provider-driven automation, scheduling, cron, or cadences.
- Adding new departments, skills, or MCP connectors.
- Parallel fan-out or large multi-agent orchestration.
- Optimizing speed, cost, or token use.
- Fixing anything discovered along the way that does not block the walk.

4. SUCCESS CRITERIA (binary, checkable — no partial credit)
S1. The goal is registered and the run is traceable to it from first step to close.
S2. Two or more distinct departments each own at least one leg, and each leg carries a task contract with acceptance criteria set before dispatch.
S3. Every leg produces a durable artifact or recorded evidence — not a claim of completion.
S4. Downstream legs demonstrably consume upstream output (handoff context present, not re-derived from scratch).
S5. At least one 🟡 action is reached, drafted in full, and stopped for human approval rather than executed.
S6. No runtime stop is bypassed, and no step reports "done" without verification the runtime recorded.
S7. The run ends with one synthesized answer and a clear status: Done / Drafted & awaiting approval / Blocked on.

5. FAILURE CONDITIONS (any one of these means the run did not prove the thing)
F1. A leg claims completion with no evidence behind it.
F2. A handoff loses context and a downstream agent restarts from the original request.
F3. A gate is skipped, auto-approved, or narrated as approved without a human yes.
F4. Scope widens mid-run into automation, new capability, or unrelated fixes.
F5. The run completes but nothing recorded lets a future session reconstruct what happened.

6. KEY ASSUMPTIONS (stated so they can be checked, not assumed true)
A1. The runtime can register a goal, carry step dependencies, and record evidence per step. (Recent work on handoff context and dependency-aware prompts suggests yes; this run tests it.)
A2. Department agents accept a task contract and return work matching its acceptance criteria.
A3. A human is reachable to answer the 🟡 gate. If not, the run legitimately ends at "Drafted & awaiting approval" — that is a pass, not a failure.
A4. Windows path and runtime cross-platform issues are resolved well enough not to block the walk. If they resurface, they are the finding, and the run reports Blocked on with the specific breakage named.

7. THE HUMAN'S DECISIONS (mine to surface, theirs to make)
D1. Which concrete request serves as the test case — I recommend one already-real piece of work over a synthetic exercise, because synthetic requests hide the handoff problems that only real ambiguity produces.
D2. Approval of the single 🟡 action when the run reaches it.
D3. Whether a partial pass (five of seven criteria, with named gaps) is enough to unlock automation work, or whether the bar is all seven.

8. DEFINITION OF DONE FOR THIS GOAL
Done means: one request, two-plus departments, every leg evidenced, one gate held, one synthesized answer, and a record complete enough that a future session can read it and know exactly what was proven and what was not. Anything less is reported as Blocked on, with the specific criterion that failed and why — because a clean negative result here is more valuable than an unclear positive.

9. WHAT THIS UNLOCKS
Passing this gate is the precondition for provider-driven automation. Automation repeats a loop; this run establishes that the loop is worth repeating. The exception-based autonomy target the company is aiming at depends on trusting the loop enough to only look at exceptions — and that trust has to be earned once, by hand, before it can be delegated to a scheduler.

STATUS: Done — goal framed, scope bounded, criteria made binary, human decisions named.
